# {{ app_slug }} — operator guide

This machine runs {{ app_slug }} via Apache (public, port 80) reverse-proxying
to mod_wsgi-express (port 8090), backed by MariaDB. The app's Python
environment is a conda env managed by Miniforge, not a system install.

This file is regenerated on every deploy — local edits will be overwritten.

## Apache2 (public reverse proxy)

    sudo systemctl start apache2
    sudo systemctl stop apache2
    sudo systemctl restart apache2
    sudo systemctl status apache2

## MariaDB (database)

    sudo systemctl start mariadb
    sudo systemctl stop mariadb
    sudo systemctl restart mariadb
    sudo systemctl status mariadb

## mod_wsgi-express (runs the {{ app_slug }} app on :8090)

This is not a systemd service — it's managed by its own apachectl wrapper:

    sudo -u {{ app_user }} /etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache/apachectl start
    sudo -u {{ app_user }} /etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache/apachectl stop
    sudo -u {{ app_user }} /etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache/apachectl restart

To rebuild the instance itself (e.g. after changing WSGI options, the conda
env, or if `--server-root` needs regenerating) rather than just
starting/stopping it, re-run `setup-server`:

    sudo -u {{ app_user }} /etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache/apachectl stop
    sudo -u {{ app_user }} bash -c 'cd {{ app_dir }} && {{ conda_dir }}/envs/{{ app_slug }}/bin/mod_wsgi-express setup-server {{ app_dir }}/{{ app_slug }}/wsgi.py --port=8090 --user {{ app_user }} --group {{ app_user }} --server-root=/etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache --envvars-script /home/{{ app_user }}/env --allow-localhost --reload-on-changes'
    sudo -u {{ app_user }} /etc/{{ app_slug }}-apache/apachectl start

This is the same command the `install-webapp` Ansible role runs on every
deploy (see `roles/install-webapp/tasks/main.yml`), so re-deploying has the
same effect.

## Conda environment ({{ app_slug }})

Miniforge is installed at {{ conda_dir }}. To run app management commands
(shell, migrations, etc.) inside the app's conda environment:

    sudo -u {{ app_user }} -i
    source {{ conda_dir }}/etc/profile.d/conda.sh
    conda activate {{ app_slug }}
    source /home/{{ app_user }}/env
    cd {{ app_dir }}
    python manage.py <command>

`source /home/{{ app_user }}/env` is required for commands that touch the
database (e.g. `migrate`, `import_transients`) — without it, DJANGO_ENV is
unset and settings.py silently falls back to a local SQLite database
instead of MariaDB.

## Load the test transient sample

Follow the conda environment steps above, then run:

    python manage.py import_transients

With no arguments this loads the bundled sample
(`apps/transients/data/sherlog_transient_sample.csv`, shipped with the app).
Safe to re-run — it upserts on (name, origin_url), so re-running just
no-ops on unchanged rows. Add `--dry-run` to validate the file without
writing anything.
